<h3>autopilot's five-phase progression (in detail)</h3>
<p>When autopilot receives a request it automatically goes through the following five phases. Each phase only starts once the previous one is finished, and parallel processing happens inside the execution and verification phases.</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Expansion</strong> — the analyst and architect unfold the short request into a detailed specification (spec.md). If it is vague, they recommend deep-interview first.</li>
    <li><strong>Planning</strong> — the architect draws up an implementation plan and a critic verifies the missing parts and the risks.</li>
    <li><strong>Execution</strong> — with ralph + ultrawork, executors matched to each difficulty level implement in parallel.</li>
    <li><strong>QA</strong> — build · lint · test are repeated up to 5 times. If the same error repeats 3 times, it treats it as a root problem and stops and reports.</li>
    <li><strong>Verification</strong> — approval from all three of architect · security-reviewer · code-reviewer is required, and rejected items are re-verified after being fixed.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Supporting modes</h3>
<p>These are the modes that complement the four core modes. They back up the before and after of execution — the planning stage, organizing requirements, root-cause analysis.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Usage example</th><th>Recommended situation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Plan</strong></td><td>Draw up a plan before execution and get user approval</td><td><code>/oh-my-claudecode:plan &lt;task&gt;</code></td><td>When you want to settle the steps and the scope before implementing (omc-plan is a compatibility alias)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Ralplan</strong></td><td>Consensus-based planning — an alias of <code>plan --consensus</code>, in which planner · architect · critic refine the plan until they agree</td><td><code>/oh-my-claudecode:ralplan &lt;description&gt;</code></td><td>Reviewing high-risk designs such as architecture · DB · security (<code>--deliberate</code> for high-risk deliberation mode)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Deep Interview</strong></td><td>Clarify vague requirements with Socratic questioning</td><td><code>/deep-interview &lt;idea&gt;</code></td><td>When the requirements are abstract or the scope is unclear — autopilot takes over and uses the resulting specification</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>UltraQA</strong></td><td>Repeat the diagnose · fix cycle until build · lint · test pass</td><td><code>/oh-my-claudecode:ultraqa &lt;goal&gt;</code></td><td>When implementation is finished and only the quality gates need to be run repeatedly</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Trace</strong></td><td>Several tracers form hypotheses in parallel and track the cause on an evidence basis</td><td><code>/oh-my-claudecode:trace</code></td><td>Root-cause analysis of outages, bugs, performance degradation</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>CCG</strong></td><td>Synthesize the results of the three models Claude · Codex · Gemini</td><td><code>/oh-my-claudecode:ccg &lt;task&gt;</code></td><td>Reviews · analyses that need multiple model perspectives</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Practical selection criteria</span>
    For a single bug or a single-file fix, ordinary conversation is enough. If you have to change several files use <code>autopilot</code>, if there are many independent tasks <code>ultrawork</code>, if a completion guarantee matters <code>ralph</code>, and if the requirements themselves are vague, using <code>deep-interview</code> first is the realistic approach. If implementation is finished and you only want to run the quality gates, use <code>ultraqa</code>; for big work that needs role division you can go all the way up to <code>team</code> — but remember that as the intensity rises the cost rises with it.
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Korean keyword support</span>
    The latest version also recognizes Korean magic keywords. If you use Korean expressions such as "랄프", "오토파일럿", "울트라워크", "코드 리뷰", "보안 리뷰", or "딥 분석" in your prompt, the same mode as the English keyword is switched on automatically. However, the Korean aliases are matched as substrings, so a mode may switch on unintentionally, and the cancel keywords (<code>stopomc</code> / <code>cancelomc</code>) only work in English.
</div>
